\lessonlabel{1}
\section{What Accounting Is}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define the purpose of accounting and explain why it matters;
  \item identify the main groups that use financial information and describe what each group needs;
  \item write the accounting equation, explain each component, and show how it stays balanced after transactions.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Purpose of accounting}

\term{Accounting} is the systematic process of recording, classifying, summarising, and interpreting the financial transactions of an entity.  
The purpose can be broken into three linked ideas:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Information provision.} Accounting creates a factual record of what has happened financially. This record is the basis for reports such as the profit‑and‑loss statement and the balance sheet.
  \item \textbf{Decision support.} Reliable information allows owners, managers, and external parties to evaluate performance, assess risk, and plan future actions. Without accurate numbers, decisions are based on guesswork.
  \item \textbf{Accountability and control.} By documenting every inflow and outflow, accounting makes it possible to check that resources are used as intended and that legal or contractual obligations are met.
\end{enumerate}

A common mistake is to treat accounting as merely bookkeeping -- the act of entering numbers into a ledger. Bookkeeping is only the first step; accounting adds classification, summarisation, and interpretation, turning raw data into useful knowledge.

\begin{example}
A local coffee shop records each sale, each purchase of coffee beans, and each wage payment in a daily journal. At month‑end the accountant groups the sales by product, subtracts the cost of beans and wages, and prepares a profit‑and‑loss statement. The owner can see that latte sales generate the highest margin and can decide whether to promote that item further.
\end{example}

\subsection{Users of financial information}

Financial information is not created for a single audience. Different users need different pieces of the same reports.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Owners and investors} -- need to know whether the business is profitable and whether their capital is growing. They focus on net income, return on equity, and cash flow.
  \item \term{Creditors and lenders} -- examine the ability of the business to meet its debt obligations. They look closely at current assets, current liabilities, and interest coverage ratios.
  \item \term{Management} -- uses detailed internal reports to plan production, control costs, set prices, and evaluate performance against budgets.
  \item \term{Government and regulators} -- require accurate tax bases, compliance with reporting standards, and evidence of lawful operation. They rely on taxable profit and statutory disclosures.
  \item \term{Employees and unions} -- are interested in the firm's stability, wage‑payment capacity, and potential for profit‑sharing. They may look at overall profitability and cash reserves.
\end{itemize}

A frequent error is to assume that a single financial statement satisfies all users. In practice, each group extracts different ratios or sections of the statements to meet its specific needs.

\begin{example}
Before granting a line of credit, a bank reviews a company's balance sheet and cash‑flow statement. The balance sheet shows that the company has \$150,000 of cash and accounts receivable, while the cash‑flow statement confirms that these cash inflows are recurring, not one‑off events. This combination reassures the bank that the firm can service the loan.
\end{example}

\subsection{The accounting equation}

The core logical structure of accounting is the \term{accounting equation}:

\[
\text{Assets} = \text{Liabilities} + \text{Equity}
\]

Each term is defined as follows:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Assets} -- resources owned or controlled by the entity that are expected to provide future economic benefits (cash, inventory, equipment, etc.).
  \item \textbf{Liabilities} -- present obligations to outsiders that will require an outflow of resources (loans, accounts payable, taxes owed).
  \item \textbf{Equity} -- the residual interest of the owners after all liabilities have been deducted; it represents the owners' claim on the assets.
\end{itemize}

The equation must hold after every transaction because every event either adds a resource, creates a claim on a resource, or transfers ownership between the three categories. Understanding how each type of transaction affects the equation is essential for accurate recording.

\textbf{Step‑by‑step reasoning for a typical transaction:}

1. Identify which accounts are affected.  
2. Determine whether each account increases or decreases.  
3. Apply the double‑entry rule: total debits must equal total credits, which guarantees that the equation stays balanced.

\textbf{Common mistake:} Recording only one side of a transaction (for example, increasing cash without recording the corresponding increase in liability or equity). This creates an imbalance that will be detected when the trial balance is prepared.

\begin{example}
A consulting firm receives \$8,000 in cash for services performed on account.  
\begin{itemize}
  \item Cash (an asset) increases by \$8,000.  
  \item Accounts receivable (also an asset) decreases by \$8,000 because the client now owes cash instead of a promise to pay.  
\end{itemize}
The net effect on total assets is zero, and liabilities and equity are unchanged, so the equation remains balanced.
\end{example}

\begin{example}
The same firm purchases a new laptop for \$2,000, paying half in cash and financing the rest with a short‑term loan.  
\begin{itemize}
  \item Laptop (asset) increases by \$2,000.  
  \item Cash (asset) decreases by \$1,000.  
  \item Short‑term loan (liability) increases by \$1,000.  
\end{itemize}
Assets increase by \$1,000, liabilities increase by the same amount, and equity is unchanged, preserving the equation.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} After recording any transaction, add the values of all asset accounts and compare the total to the sum of all liability and equity accounts. The two totals must be equal; any discrepancy indicates an error in the double‑entry recording.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Explain in one sentence why providing reliable profit information is a core purpose of accounting.
  \item List three different users of financial statements and state one specific piece of information each user typically requires.
  \item A company issues \$20,000 of new shares to investors. Show how this transaction affects the accounting equation.
  \item The firm pays \$3,500 cash to settle part of a bank loan. Record the impact on assets, liabilities, and equity.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.5cm}p{11.5cm}@{}}
\hline
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\hline
Accounting & Systematic recording, classifying, summarising, and interpreting of financial transactions. \\
Purpose of accounting & To provide reliable information for decision‑making, to enable accountability, and to support control of resources. \\
Users of financial information & Groups that rely on accounting reports, such as owners, creditors, management, government, and employees. \\
Assets & Economic resources owned or controlled by an entity that are expected to generate future benefits. \\
Liabilities & Present obligations that will require an outflow of resources to settle. \\
Equity & The residual interest of owners after deducting liabilities from assets. \\
Accounting equation & The identity Assets = Liabilities + Equity that must hold after every transaction. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{2}
\section{Recording Transactions}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item recognise and describe the main types of source documents used in business accounting;
  \item explain why double‑entry bookkeeping is required and how it keeps the accounting equation in balance;
  \item define \term{debit} and \term{credit}, state how each affects the five major account types, and apply the rules to a range of typical transactions;
  \item avoid common mistakes such as reversing debits and credits or omitting a second entry.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Source documents}

\term{Source documents} are the original pieces of evidence that a transaction has taken place. They may be paper‑based (invoices, receipts) or electronic (e‑mails, PDFs). A reliable accounting system never records a transaction without first locating a source document, because the document provides the audit trail required for verification and for external review.

Typical source documents include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Sales invoice}: issued by a seller to a buyer; shows date, buyer name, items sold, unit price, total amount, tax, and payment terms.
  \item \textbf{Purchase receipt}: issued by a supplier; records date, supplier name, goods or services received, amount paid, and method of payment.
  \item \textbf{Bank statement}: monthly summary from a bank; lists all deposits, withdrawals, fees, and interest for the period.
  \item \textbf{Cash register tape}: printed record from a point‑of‑sale system; details each cash sale, time, and amount.
  \item \textbf{Payroll slip}: prepared by the payroll department; shows employee name, pay period, gross wages, deductions, and net pay.
\end{itemize}

Each document must contain at least four pieces of information:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Date} -- when the transaction occurred;
  \item \textbf{Parties} -- who gave and who received value;
  \item \textbf{Amount} -- monetary value, often broken into sub‑totals;
  \item \textbf{Description} -- brief narrative of the transaction.
\end{enumerate}
The accountant extracts these data points and enters them into the ledger or accounting software. Missing or incomplete source documents are a common source of error; always verify that the document is authentic and complete before posting.

\begin{example}
A catering company receives a delivery of fresh produce. The delivery note (a source document) reads:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Date: 5 April 2026
  \item Supplier: Green Farms Ltd.
  \item Items: 20 kg tomatoes, 15 kg lettuce, 10 kg carrots
  \item Unit price: \$2 per kg (tomatoes), \$1.5 per kg (lettuce), \$1 per kg (carrots)
  \item Total amount: \$85
\end{itemize}
The accountant records a purchase of inventory worth \$85, using the delivery note as proof of receipt.
\end{example}

\subsection{Double‑entry bookkeeping}

\term{Double‑entry bookkeeping} is the foundation of modern accounting. The principle states that every economic event must be recorded in at least two accounts: one account receives a \term{debit} entry, another receives a \term{credit} entry. This ensures that the fundamental accounting equation remains true after each posting:
\[
\text{Assets} = \text{Liabilities} + \text{Equity}
\]

Why two entries? When a company acquires an asset, it must give up something of equal value---cash, a liability, or equity. Recording both sides makes the flow of resources transparent and provides built‑in error checking: if total debits do not equal total credits, the transaction is incomplete or incorrectly posted.

The process for a new transaction is:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify the economic event and locate the source document.
  \item Determine which accounts are affected (e.g., cash, inventory, revenue).
  \item Decide which account is increased and which is decreased, using the debit/credit rules.
  \item Write the journal entry, listing the debit first, then the credit, with equal monetary amounts.
  \item Post the amounts to the respective ledger accounts.
\end{enumerate}

Common mistakes at this stage include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Omitting the second entry}: posting only a debit or only a credit breaks the equation.
  \item \textbf{Reversing the direction}: debiting an account that should be credited (or vice‑versa) leads to misstated balances.
  \item \textbf{Using the wrong account type}: for example, recording a cash receipt as a debit to \textit{Accounts Payable} instead of \textit{Cash}.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A consulting firm receives a \$2,500 cash payment for services rendered. The journal entry is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Debit \textit{Cash} \$2,500 (asset increase)
  \item Credit \textit{Service Revenue} \$2,500 (equity increase)
\end{itemize}
Debits equal credits, and the equation stays balanced: assets rise by \$2,500, equity rises by the same amount.
\end{example}

\subsection{Debits and credits}

The terms \term{debit} (Dr) and \term{credit} (Cr) describe the two sides of every account. Their effect depends on the account classification. The five major account types are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Assets}: resources owned (cash, inventory, equipment).
  \item \textbf{Liabilities}: obligations to outsiders (accounts payable, loans).
  \item \textbf{Equity}: owners' residual interest (capital, retained earnings).
  \item \textbf{Revenue}: inflows from ordinary activities (sales, fees).
  \item \textbf{Expenses}: outflows incurred to earn revenue (rent, utilities).
\end{itemize}

The debit/credit rules are:

\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|}
\hline
Account type & Debit effect & Credit effect \\
\hline
Assets & increase & decrease \\
Liabilities & decrease & increase \\
Equity & decrease & increase \\
Revenue & decrease & increase \\
Expenses & increase & decrease \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

To apply the rules, follow these steps for each transaction:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item List all accounts involved.
  \item Classify each account (asset, liability, etc.).
  \item Decide whether the account is increasing or decreasing.
  \item Apply the appropriate debit or credit based on the table above.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
The firm purchases a computer on credit for \$1,200.
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textit{Computer Equipment} (asset) increases $\rightarrow$ Debit \$1,200.
  \item \textit{Accounts Payable} (liability) increases $\rightarrow$ Credit \$1,200.
\end{itemize}
The entry balances and reflects that the firm now owes the supplier while gaining an asset.
\end{example}

A frequent error is to treat ``credit'' as a synonym for ``negative'' in all contexts. In reality, a credit to an asset account reduces the asset, while a credit to a liability or equity account increases it. Remember to consult the rule table rather than rely on intuition.

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Record the following transaction using double‑entry bookkeeping: The firm pays \$350 cash for a monthly internet service bill.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify three source documents that would support a cash purchase of office furniture and list the four required data items each must contain.
  \item For each scenario, write the correct debit and credit accounts with amounts:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item The company sells services on account for \$4,200.
      \item The company receives a \$5,000 bank loan and deposits the cash in the bank.
      \item The company pays \$600 cash for advertising expenses.
    \end{enumerate}
  \item Explain, in your own words, why the double‑entry system prevents the accounting equation from becoming unbalanced.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{|p{3.5cm}|p{10.5cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\hline
Source document & Original paper or electronic record that proves a transaction occurred; contains date, parties, amount, and description. \\
\hline
Double‑entry bookkeeping & System requiring at least two accounts per transaction, with equal debits and credits, to keep the accounting equation balanced. \\
\hline
Debit (Dr) & Left‑hand entry; increases assets and expenses, decreases liabilities, equity, and revenue. \\
\hline
Credit (Cr) & Right‑hand entry; increases liabilities, equity, and revenue, decreases assets and expenses. \\
\hline
Asset & Resource owned or controlled by the entity that is expected to provide future economic benefit. \\
\hline
Liability & Present obligation arising from past events, settlement of which is expected to result in an outflow of resources. \\
\hline
Equity & Owner's residual interest after liabilities are deducted from assets. \\
\hline
Revenue & Inflows from the ordinary activities of the entity that increase equity. \\
\hline
Expense & Outflows or using up of assets that decrease equity. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

