\section{The General Ledger}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define a \term{ledger account} and explain its structure and purpose;
  \item describe the complete posting process for a transaction, including common mistakes to avoid;
  \item calculate the balance of any ledger account and identify whether it is a debit or credit balance;
  \item compare the general ledger to subsidiary records and understand its role in financial reporting.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Ledger accounts}

A \term{ledger account} is a permanent record that collects all debit and credit entries for one specific element of the accounting equation: an asset, a liability, equity, revenue or expense. Each account is presented in a two‑column format. The left column is the \term{debit side}; the right column is the \term{credit side}.  

The purpose of a ledger account is to show, over time, how a single item changes and what its current balance is. By aggregating entries from many transactions, the ledger provides the raw data for the trial balance and the financial statements.

Key features of a ledger account:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Date column}: records the date of each transaction.
  \item \textbf{Reference column}: often a journal page number or a transaction code.
  \item \textbf{Description column}: brief note of the source document.
  \item \textbf{Debit column}: amounts that increase assets or expenses, or decrease liabilities, equity or revenue.
  \item \textbf{Credit column}: amounts that increase liabilities, equity or revenue, or decrease assets or expenses.
\end{itemize}

A common mistake is to place an amount on the wrong side of the account. For example, recording a cash receipt as a credit in the Cash account will produce an incorrect balance and later cause the trial balance to be out of balance.

\begin{example}
The Cash account is opened with a beginning balance of \$0.  
\begin{itemize}
  \item 01 Jan: cash sale of \$500 -- entered as a debit of \$500 on the left.
  \item 05 Jan: payment of a utility bill of \$120 -- entered as a credit of \$120 on the right.
\end{itemize}
Totals: Debit \$500, Credit \$120. Net balance = \$380 debit, shown on the left side as the closing balance.
\end{example}

\subsection{Posting transactions}

\term{Posting} is the act of transferring each amount from a journal entry to the appropriate ledger accounts. Every transaction must satisfy the double‑entry rule: total debits equal total credits. The posting process ensures that each affected account reflects the transaction in its own record.

The detailed steps are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Identify all accounts involved}. Read the journal entry and list each account name.
  \item \textbf{Classify each amount as debit or credit}. Use the normal balance rules for each account type.
  \item \textbf{Locate the ledger page for each account}. Open the T‑account or electronic ledger file.
  \item \textbf{Enter the date, reference and description}. This creates an audit trail.
  \item \textbf{Write the amount in the correct column}. Debit amounts go on the left, credit amounts on the right.
  \item \textbf{Update the running totals}. Many systems calculate the subtotal automatically; on paper you add the new amount to the existing total.
\end{enumerate}

Typical errors during posting:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Reversing debit and credit sides.
  \item Omitting the reference number, making later verification difficult.
  \item Forgetting to post to all accounts, which leaves the trial balance out of balance.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A company buys equipment on credit for \$2{,}000. The journal entry is:
\[
\text{Equipment}\ \text{Debit}\ \$2{,}000 \\
\text{Accounts Payable}\ \text{Credit}\ \$2{,}000
\]
Posting steps:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify accounts: Equipment (asset) and Accounts Payable (liability).
  \item Debit Equipment: write \$2{,}000 on the left side of the Equipment ledger.
  \item Credit Accounts Payable: write \$2{,}000 on the right side of the Accounts Payable ledger.
  \item Record date (e.g., 12 Feb) and reference (Journal page 15) in both accounts.
\end{enumerate}
After posting, the Equipment account shows a debit balance of \$2{,}000, and Accounts Payable shows a credit balance of the same amount.
\end{example}

\subsection{Balancing an account}

Balancing determines the net amount that remains in an account after all entries for a period have been recorded. The procedure is the same for every account type.

\[
\text{Balance} = 
\begin{cases}
\text{Total Debits} - \text{Total Credits}, & \text{if Total Debits} > \text{Total Credits (debit balance)}\\[4pt]
\text{Total Credits} - \text{Total Debits}, & \text{if Total Credits} > \text{Total Debits (credit balance)}
\end{cases}
\]

Steps to balance:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Sum all debit entries in the account.
  \item Sum all credit entries in the account.
  \item Compare the two totals.
  \item Write the difference on the side with the larger total and label it ``Balance c/f'' (carried forward).
\end{enumerate}

A frequent mistake is to subtract the larger total from the smaller one, which yields a negative number and reverses the balance type. Always subtract the smaller total from the larger total.

\begin{example}
The Supplies account contains:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Total debits = \$800
  \item Total credits = \$300
\end{itemize}
Because debits are larger, the balance is a debit of \$500 (\$800‑\$300). The closing entry is recorded on the left side as ``Balance c/f \$500''.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Given the journal entry below, perform the full posting process and compute the balance of each affected ledger account.

Journal entry (date 20 Mar):
\[
\text{Service Revenue}\ \text{Credit}\ \$1{,}200 \\
\text{Accounts Receivable}\ \text{Debit}\ \$1{,}200
\]

List the accounts, indicate the side for each amount, show the posted lines with date and reference, and calculate the resulting balances.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Ledger account & Permanent record that accumulates all debits and credits for one specific item (asset, liability, equity, revenue or expense). \\
Debit & Entry recorded on the left side; increases assets and expenses, decreases liabilities, equity and revenue. \\
Credit & Entry recorded on the right side; increases liabilities, equity and revenue, decreases assets and expenses. \\
Posting & The systematic transfer of amounts from journal entries to the appropriate ledger accounts. \\
Balance & Net amount left after comparing total debits and total credits; shown as either a debit or credit balance. \\
Double‑entry & Accounting principle that every transaction affects at least two accounts with equal total debits and credits. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

