\section{Adjusting Entries}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item explain why adjusting entries are required at the end of an accounting period;
  \item record accruals for revenues earned and expenses incurred but not yet received or paid;
  \item record prepayments for cash received or paid in advance of the related revenue or expense;
  \item calculate and post depreciation expense for a depreciable asset;
  \item estimate and record bad‑debt expense using the allowance method.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Why adjusting entries are needed}

Financial statements must reflect the economic activity of the period they cover. Transactions that cross the period boundary are not fully captured by the cash‑based journal entries made during the period. Adjusting entries move amounts from the balance sheet to the income statement (or vice‑versa) so that revenue and expense are matched to the period in which they arise. The result is a set of statements that obey the accrual basis of accounting.

\subsection{Accruals}

\term{Accruals} record revenues that have been earned or expenses that have been incurred before cash changes hands.

\subsubsection{Accrued revenue}

If a service is performed on 31 March but the client will pay on 15 April, the revenue belongs to March. The adjusting entry on 31 March debits \term{Accounts receivable} and credits \term{Service revenue}.

\begin{example}
A consulting firm completed a \$3,200 project on 30 March. The client will pay on 15 April. The adjusting entry on 31 March is:
\[
\text{Debit Accounts receivable } \$3{,}200 \\
\text{Credit Service revenue } \$3{,}200
\]
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Accrued expense}

If employees earned wages for the last week of March but will be paid on 5 April, the expense belongs to March. The adjusting entry debits \term{Wage expense} and credits \term{Wages payable}.

\begin{example}
Wages of \$1,500 were earned by staff in the final week of March and will be paid on 5 April. The adjusting entry on 31 March is:
\[
\text{Debit Wage expense } \$1{,}500 \\
\text{Credit Wages payable } \$1{,}500
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Prepayments}

\term{Prepayments} (or deferrals) record cash received or paid before the related revenue or expense is earned or incurred. The initial cash entry creates an asset (prepaid expense) or a liability (unearned revenue). The adjusting entry transfers the appropriate portion to the income statement.

\subsubsection{Prepaid expense}

A company pays \$12,000 on 1 January for a one‑year insurance policy. Each month \$1,000 of insurance expense is recognised. The adjusting entry on 31 January is:

\begin{example}
\[
\text{Debit Insurance expense } \$1{,}000 \\
\text{Credit Prepaid insurance } \$1{,}000
\]
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Unearned revenue}

A gym receives \$6,000 on 1 June for a six‑month membership. Each month \$1,000 of membership revenue is earned. The adjusting entry on 30 June is:

\begin{example}
\[
\text{Debit Unearned revenue } \$1{,}000 \\
\text{Credit Membership revenue } \$1{,}000
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Depreciation}

Depreciation spreads the cost of a tangible asset over its useful life. The asset remains on the balance sheet at cost, while accumulated depreciation is a contra‑asset that reduces the book value. The adjusting entry each period records depreciation expense and increases accumulated depreciation.

\subsubsection{Straight‑line method}

The straight‑line method allocates an equal amount each period:

\[
\text{Depreciation expense} = \frac{\text{Cost} - \text{Residual value}}{\text{Useful life in periods}}
\]

\begin{example}
A delivery van costs \$30,000, has a residual value of \$5,000 and a useful life of 5 years. Annual depreciation is:
\[
\frac{30{,}000 - 5{,}000}{5} = \$5{,}000
\]
The adjusting entry at year‑end is:
\[
\text{Debit Depreciation expense } \$5{,}000 \\
\text{Credit Accumulated depreciation -- Van } \$5{,}000
\]
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Declining‑balance method}

The declining‑balance method applies a constant rate to the book value at the start of each period. The rate is usually a multiple of the straight‑line rate (e.g., double‑declining).

\begin{example}
Using double‑declining on the same van: straight‑line rate = 1/5 = 20\%. Double‑declining rate = 40\%. First‑year depreciation:
\[
0.40 \times 30{,}000 = \$12{,}000
\]
Adjusting entry:
\[
\text{Debit Depreciation expense } \$12{,}000 \\
\text{Credit Accumulated depreciation -- Van } \$12{,}000
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Bad Debts}

When some accounts receivable are unlikely to be collected, an expense must be recognised. The allowance method estimates uncollectible amounts and records a contra‑asset \term{Allowance for doubtful accounts}.

\subsubsection{Estimating bad‑debt expense}

A common estimate is a percentage of credit sales. Suppose credit sales for the month are \$50,000 and the company expects 2\% will be uncollectible.

\begin{example}
Bad‑debt expense = 0.02 × 50,000 = \$1,000. The adjusting entry is:
\[
\text{Debit Bad‑debt expense } \$1{,}000 \\
\text{Credit Allowance for doubtful accounts } \$1{,}000
\]
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Writing off specific accounts}

When a particular receivable is identified as uncollectible, the allowance is reduced and the receivable is removed.

\begin{example}
A customer owing \$600 is deemed uncollectible. The entry is:
\[
\text{Debit Allowance for doubtful accounts } \$600 \\
\text{Credit Accounts receivable } \$600
\]
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} For each of the following situations decide which type of adjusting entry is required and write the journal entry:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Services performed worth \$2,800 on 31 December, payment due 10 January.
  \item Rent paid on 1 July for a twelve‑month lease; adjust for the month of July.
  \item A machine costing \$18,000 with a residual value of \$3,000 and a five‑year straight‑line life; record year‑end depreciation.
  \item Credit sales of \$40,000 with an estimated 3\% uncollectible; record bad‑debt expense.
\end{enumerate}
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Accrual & Revenue earned or expense incurred before cash is received or paid. \\
Prepayment & Cash received or paid before the related revenue or expense is earned or incurred. \\
Depreciation & Systematic allocation of a tangible asset's cost over its useful life. \\
Allowance for doubtful accounts & Contra‑asset that estimates the portion of receivables expected to be uncollectible. \\
Bad‑debt expense & Expense recognised for estimated uncollectible accounts receivable. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{6}
\section{The Income Statement}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{revenues} and explain how they are recognised;
  \item list the main categories of \term{expenses} and describe the timing of their recognition;
  \item calculate \term{gross profit} and interpret what it tells about a firm's core operations;
  \item calculate \term{net profit} and discuss why it is the key indicator of overall profitability;
  \item identify common mistakes when preparing an income statement and avoid them.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Revenues}

\term{Revenues} are the inflow of economic benefits that arise from the ordinary activities of an entity during a reporting period. They represent the amount earned from selling goods, providing services, or other primary operations. Revenue is recognised when the performance obligation is satisfied, i.e., when the customer obtains control of the promised good or service, and the amount can be measured reliably. This is the core of the accrual basis of accounting.

Common mistakes:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Including cash advances that have not yet been earned as revenue.
  \item Counting sales that are later cancelled or returned without adjusting the figure.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A software company sells a one‑year licence for \$12{,}000 on 1 July. By 30 September it has delivered the software and the customer can use it. The revenue recognised for the quarter is \$12{,}000, because the performance obligation is fulfilled at the start of the licence period.
\end{example}

\subsection{Expenses}

\term{Expenses} are the outflows or de‑creasings of economic benefits that result from the entity's efforts to generate revenue. They include the cost of goods sold (COGS), salaries, rent, utilities, depreciation, interest, and taxes. Under accrual accounting, an expense is recorded when the related benefit is consumed, not necessarily when cash is paid.

Categories:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Cost of goods sold (COGS)} -- direct costs of producing the goods sold or services delivered.
  \item \textbf{Operating expenses} -- selling, general and administrative costs such as wages, advertising, and rent.
  \item \textbf{Non‑operating expenses} -- interest expense, loss on sale of assets, etc.
  \item \textbf{Tax expense} -- income taxes payable for the period.
\end{itemize}

Common mistakes:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Mixing capital expenditures (which should be capitalised) with expenses.
  \item Forgetting to accrue for utilities or wages that have been incurred but not yet paid.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A manufacturing firm incurs the following costs in a month: raw material \$8{,}000, direct labour \$5{,}000, factory overhead allocated \$2{,}000, office rent \$1{,}200, and interest on a loan \$300. The total expenses for the month are \$8{,}000 + \$5{,}000 + \$2{,}000 + \$1{,}200 + \$300 = \$16{,}500.
\end{example}

\subsection{Gross profit}

\term{Gross profit} measures the profitability of a company's core production or service activities before accounting for operating and non‑operating costs. It is calculated as the difference between revenues and the cost of goods sold.

\[
\text{Gross profit} = \text{Revenues} - \text{Cost of goods sold}
\]

Gross profit indicates how efficiently a firm turns inputs into sales. A higher gross profit margin suggests better control of production costs or stronger pricing power.

Common mistake: using total expenses instead of COGS in the calculation, which understates the true gross profit.

\begin{example}
A clothing retailer records revenue of \$120{,}000 for the quarter. The cost of the inventory sold (COGS) is \$70{,}000. Gross profit equals \$120{,}000 - \$70{,}000 = \$50{,}000.
\end{example}

\subsection{Net profit}

\term{Net profit}, also called net income, is the residual amount after all expenses, including operating costs, interest, and taxes, have been deducted from revenues. It reflects the overall financial performance of the entity for the reporting period.

\[
\text{Net profit} = \text{Revenues} - \text{All expenses}
\]

Net profit is the figure used to calculate earnings per share and to assess the ability of a business to generate returns for its owners. It is also the basis for retained earnings in the equity section of the balance sheet.

Common mistakes:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Omitting tax expense, which can significantly reduce the net figure.
  \item Double‑counting depreciation by recording it both as an expense and as a reduction of asset value without proper adjustment.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
Continuing the retailer example, suppose the same quarter incurs operating expenses of \$30{,}000, interest expense of \$2{,}000, and tax expense of \$5{,}000. Total expenses equal COGS (\$70{,}000) + operating (\$30{,}000) + interest (\$2{,}000) + tax (\$5{,}000) = \$107{,}000. Net profit = \$120{,}000 - \$107{,}000 = \$13{,}000.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} A technology firm reports revenue of \$250{,}000, cost of goods sold of \$150{,}000, operating expenses of \$40{,}000, interest expense of \$5{,}000 and tax expense of \$12{,}000. Compute its gross profit and net profit.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Revenues & Inflows earned from the entity's primary business activities during the reporting period. \\
Expenses & Outflows or de‑creasings of economic benefits incurred to generate revenues; classified as COGS, operating, non‑operating, and tax expenses. \\
Gross profit & Revenue minus cost of goods sold; indicates profitability of core operations before other costs. \\
Net profit & Revenue minus all expenses; the final profit figure that shows overall profitability after all costs and taxes. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{7}
\section{The Balance Sheet}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{assets}, \term{liabilities} and \term{equity} with full explanations;
  \item distinguish \term{current} from \term{non‑current} items for each of the three sections;
  \item describe how the three sections are linked by the accounting equation and why the equation must always balance;
  \item recognise common classification mistakes and correct them;
  \item interpret a simple balance sheet in a real‑world business context.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Assets}

\term{Assets} are economic resources owned or controlled by an entity that are expected to produce future benefits. Benefits may be cash inflows, cost savings, or the ability to produce goods and services. Assets are recorded on the left side (or top) of the balance sheet because they represent what the business possesses.

Two major dimensions are used to classify assets:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Current assets}: items that will be realised in cash, sold, or consumed within the normal operating cycle---usually one year. Typical current assets are cash, cash equivalents, accounts receivable, inventory, short‑term investments, and prepaid expenses. The key test is timing: if the company expects to turn the item into cash or use it up in less than twelve months, it is current.
  \item \term{Non‑current assets}: items that provide economic benefits for more than one year. These include property, plant and equipment (PPE), intangible assets such as patents and goodwill, long‑term investments, and deferred tax assets. Non‑current assets are often depreciated or amortised over their useful lives, reflecting the gradual consumption of the benefit.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Step‑by‑step classification}
1. Identify the nature of the resource (cash, inventory, equipment, etc.).  
2. Estimate the period over which the resource will be used or converted.  
3. If the period is $\leq$ 12 months, place the item under current assets; otherwise, classify it as non‑current.  

\paragraph{Common mistake}
Treating a long‑term loan that the company intends to repay within the next six months as a current asset. A loan is a liability, not an asset; the correct test is whether the resource is owned, not whether a related obligation is short‑term.

\begin{example}
A software firm holds the following at year‑end:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Cash: \$25,000 (current);
  \item Accounts receivable: \$40,000 expected to be collected in 45 days (current);
  \item Office furniture: \$15,000 with an estimated useful life of 10 years (non‑current);
  \item Patent on a proprietary algorithm: \$60,000 with legal protection for 20 years (non‑current).
\end{itemize}
The balance sheet lists cash and receivables in the current‑asset section, while furniture and the patent appear in the non‑current‑asset section.
\end{example}

\subsection{Liabilities}

\term{Liabilities} are present obligations of the entity arising from past events, the settlement of which is expected to result in an outflow of resources (usually cash). Liabilities are recorded on the right side (or bottom) of the balance sheet because they represent claims against the assets.

Liabilities are also divided by timing:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Current liabilities}: obligations that must be settled within one year. Examples are accounts payable, short‑term bank borrowings, the current portion of long‑term debt, accrued expenses, and taxes payable. The current portion of a long‑term loan is the amount due in the next twelve months.
  \item \term{Non‑current liabilities}: obligations that are not due within the next year. Typical items include long‑term bank loans, bonds payable, lease liabilities with a term longer than one year, and deferred tax liabilities.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Step‑by‑step classification}
1. Identify the nature of the obligation (supplier invoice, loan agreement, lease).  
2. Determine the next payment date.  
3. If payment is required within 12 months, classify as current; otherwise, classify as non‑current.  

\paragraph{Common mistake}
Including the entire balance of a five‑year loan in current liabilities. Only the portion due in the next year belongs to current liabilities; the remainder stays in non‑current liabilities.

\begin{example}
A manufacturing company reports:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Accounts payable: \$30,000 (current);
  \item Short‑term bank overdraft: \$10,000 (current);
  \item Long‑term loan: \$100,000 with \$20,000 due in the next 12 months (current portion) and the remaining \$80,000 due thereafter (non‑current);
  \item Deferred tax liability: \$15,000 (non‑current).
\end{itemize}
The balance sheet shows the \$20,000 as part of current liabilities and the \$80,000 as part of non‑current liabilities.
\end{example}

\subsection{Equity}

\term{Equity} (also called owners' equity or shareholders' equity) represents the residual interest in the assets after all liabilities have been deducted. In formula form:

\[
\text{Equity} = \text{Assets} - \text{Liabilities}.
\]

Equity is the owners' claim on the business and is shown on the right side of the balance sheet, below liabilities.

Typical components of equity are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Share capital}: cash or other assets contributed by owners in exchange for ownership units (shares). It may be divided into common and preferred share capital.
  \item \term{Retained earnings}: cumulative net profit that has been kept in the business rather than distributed as dividends. Retained earnings increase when the company earns profit and decrease when it pays dividends or incurs losses.
  \item \term{Other reserves}: such as revaluation surplus, foreign‑currency translation reserve, or treasury shares. These items arise from specific accounting rules and are less common in introductory examples.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Step‑by‑step calculation}
1. Sum all asset values.  
2. Sum all liability values.  
3. Subtract total liabilities from total assets.  
4. The result is the total equity, which is then broken down into its components.

\paragraph{Common mistake}
Treating dividends paid during the year as an expense that reduces profit, then also subtracting them from equity. Dividends are a distribution of equity; they reduce retained earnings directly, not profit.

\begin{example}
A start‑up reports total assets of \$250,000 and total liabilities of \$150,000. Equity therefore equals \$100,000. The equity section is broken down as:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Share capital: \$60,000;
  \item Retained earnings: \$35,000;
  \item Treasury shares (cost of shares repurchased): \$(5,000).
\end{itemize}
The net equity remains \$100,000 because \$60,000 + \$35,000 -- \$5,000 = \$100,000.
\end{example}

\subsection{Current and Non‑Current Items Across the Three Sections}

The current/non‑current distinction is applied consistently to assets, liabilities and, indirectly, to equity. For equity, the classification matters only when equity includes items that are tied to time, such as share‑based payment awards that vest over several years; these are usually disclosed in notes rather than split on the face of the balance sheet.

\paragraph{Why the distinction matters}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Liquidity analysis}: Current assets versus current liabilities give the current ratio, a key indicator of short‑term solvency.
  \item \textbf{Solvency analysis}: Non‑current assets and non‑current liabilities together show the long‑term financial structure and the ability to generate future cash flows.
  \item \textbf{Investor insight}: Investors compare the proportion of non‑current assets (e.g., property, patents) to equity to assess capital intensity.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Typical layout}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Assets are listed first, split into current assets (top) and non‑current assets (bottom).
  \item Liabilities follow, again with current liabilities first and non‑current liabilities second.
  \item Equity appears last, often with share capital first, then retained earnings and other reserves.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
Consider the balance sheet of a retail chain:

\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Assets} & \\
\midrule
Current assets: & \\
\quad Cash & \$12,000 \\
\quad Accounts receivable & \$8,000 \\
\quad Inventory & \$20,000 \\
Non‑current assets: & \\
\quad Store property & \$150,000 \\
\quad Delivery trucks & \$30,000 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Liabilities} & \\
\midrule
Current liabilities: & \\
\quad Accounts payable & \$15,000 \\
\quad Short‑term loan & \$5,000 \\
Non‑current liabilities: & \\
\quad Mortgage on store property & \$80,000 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Equity} & \\
\midrule
Share capital & \$50,000 \\
Retained earnings & \$45,000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

The current‑asset total (\$40,000) is compared with current liabilities (\$20,000) to give a current ratio of 2.0, indicating adequate short‑term liquidity. The non‑current assets (\$180,000) are financed largely by the mortgage (\$80,000) and equity (\$95,000), showing a stable long‑term structure.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} For each line item on a balance sheet ask:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Does the item represent a resource owned by the entity? $\rightarrow$ Asset.
  \item Does the item represent an obligation to pay out resources? $\rightarrow$ Liability.
  \item Is the item the residual claim after liabilities are deducted? $\rightarrow$ Equity.
  \item Will the resource be realised or the obligation settled within twelve months? $\rightarrow$ Current; otherwise, Non‑current.
\end{enumerate}
After classification, verify that \(\text{Total Assets} = \text{Total Liabilities} + \text{Total Equity}\). Any mismatch indicates a recording error.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Classify the following as current or non‑current: (a) cash on hand, (b) a five‑year lease liability, (c) inventory, (d) a patent, (e) the portion of a ten‑year loan due in the next six months.
  \item A company reports: Assets = \$340,000; Current liabilities = \$70,000; Non‑current liabilities = \$130,000. Compute equity and state whether the accounting equation balances.
  \item Identify the error in each statement and correct it:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item ``The \$25,000 bank loan is listed as a current asset because it will be repaid within one year.''
      \item ``Retained earnings are shown under current liabilities because they are expected to be paid out as dividends within the year.''
    \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Assets & Resources owned or controlled that are expected to generate future economic benefits. \\
Current assets & Assets expected to be realised in cash, sold, or consumed within one year. \\
Non‑current assets & Assets providing benefits for more than one year; often depreciated or amortised. \\
Liabilities & Present obligations to transfer economic resources to others. \\
Current liabilities & Obligations due for settlement within one year. \\
Non‑current liabilities & Obligations due after one year. \\
Equity & Residual interest in assets after deducting liabilities; owners' claim on the business. \\
Share capital & Money or other assets contributed by owners in exchange for ownership units. \\
Retained earnings & Cumulative net profit retained in the business rather than distributed as dividends. \\
Current & Classification based on a twelve‑month horizon; indicates short‑term liquidity. \\
Non‑current & Classification based on a horizon longer than twelve months; indicates long‑term solvency. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{8}
\section{Cash Flow Basics}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define the three categories of cash‑flow activities and explain why each is important;
  \item break down typical transactions into operating, investing or financing groups, and identify the direction of cash (inflow or outflow);
  \item read a cash flow statement, follow the calculation steps, and interpret what the net cash change tells a manager or investor.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Operating activities}

\term{Operating activities} are cash movements that arise directly from a firm's core business. They reflect how the company generates cash from selling its products or services and how it spends cash to keep the operation running. Typical items include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item cash received from customers for sales of goods or services;
  \item cash paid to suppliers for inventory, raw materials or utilities;
  \item cash paid for wages, salaries and employee benefits;
  \item cash paid for taxes, interest on short‑term debt and other operating expenses;
  \item cash received from refunds or rebates related to operating costs.
\end{itemize}

A common mistake is to treat non‑cash expenses such as depreciation as operating cash flows. Depreciation reduces profit on the income statement but does not involve cash leaving the firm, so it is added back when converting profit to cash flow.

\begin{example}
A small café earns \$4{,}500 in cash sales during a week. It pays \$2{,}300 for coffee beans, \$600 for utilities, and \$700 for staff wages. The cash flow from operating activities is:
\[
4{,}500 - 2{,}300 - 600 - 700 = 900\text{ (inflow)}.
\]
If the café also recorded \$200 of depreciation expense, this amount would be added back in a full cash‑flow calculation, but it does not appear in the simple cash‑flow figure above.
\end{example}

\subsection{Investing activities}

\term{Investing activities} capture cash that is used to acquire or dispose of long‑term assets and investments. These assets are expected to generate benefits over several periods, so the cash impact is usually larger and less frequent than operating cash flows. Typical investing cash flows are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item cash paid to purchase property, plant, equipment (PPE) or intangible assets;
  \item cash received from the sale of PPE, land, buildings or other long‑term assets;
  \item cash paid for purchases of securities (stocks, bonds) of other companies;
  \item cash received from the sale of investments or the receipt of interest/dividends from those investments (when classified as investing rather than operating, depending on accounting policy).
\end{itemize}

Students often confuse the purchase of inventory with investing cash flow. Inventory is a current asset used in operations, so its purchase belongs to operating activities, not investing.

\begin{example}
A tech start‑up buys a server rack for \$12{,}000 and a software license for \$3{,}000. In the same quarter it sells an old office printer for \$500. The net cash used in investing activities is:
\[
12{,}000 + 3{,}000 - 500 = 14{,}500\text{ (outflow)}.
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Financing activities}

\term{Financing activities} record cash that changes the firm's capital structure. They show how a company raises cash from owners or creditors and how it returns cash to them. Typical financing cash flows include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item cash received from issuing new shares or bonds;
  \item cash paid to repurchase shares (treasury stock) or to retire debt;
  \item cash received from borrowing (bank loans, lines of credit);
  \item cash paid to repay principal on loans;
  \item cash paid as dividends to shareholders.
\end{itemize}

A frequent error is to treat interest paid as a financing cash flow. Under most accounting standards, interest paid is classified as an operating cash outflow because it relates to the cost of borrowing used in day‑to‑day operations.

\begin{example}
During a fiscal year a company issues \$25{,}000 of new equity, takes a bank loan of \$10{,}000, repays \$4{,}000 of an existing loan, and pays a dividend of \$3{,}000. Net cash from financing activities is:
\[
25{,}000 + 10{,}000 - 4{,}000 - 3{,}000 = 28{,}000\text{ (inflow)}.
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Reading a cash flow statement}

A cash flow statement presents the three activity sections in a fixed order: operating, investing, financing. The statement begins with the net cash provided by (or used in) operating activities, adds the net cash from investing activities, and finally adds the net cash from financing activities. The sum of the three sections equals the \term{net change in cash} for the reporting period.

Steps to read a cash flow statement:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify the cash inflows and outflows in each activity category. Verify that each line item belongs to the correct section.
  \item Calculate the net cash for each section by adding inflows and subtracting outflows.
  \item Add the three net amounts to obtain the overall change in cash.
  \item Compare the net change with the opening cash balance (often shown in the statement) to confirm the closing cash balance.
\end{enumerate}

Interpretation tips:

\begin{itemize}
  \item A positive operating cash flow indicates the core business is generating cash, which is a sign of operational health.
  \item Large negative investing cash flow may signal significant capital investment, which could be positive if it leads to future growth.
  \item Positive financing cash flow shows the firm is raising capital; negative financing cash flow may indicate debt repayment or dividend distribution.
  \item Consistently negative net cash change could threaten liquidity unless offset by a strong cash reserve.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Net cash from operating activities} &= +2{,}500 \\
\text{Net cash used in investing activities} &= -6{,}800 \\
\text{Net cash from financing activities} &= +4{,}200 \\
\hline
\text{Net change in cash} &= -200 \\
\text{Opening cash balance} &= 5{,}000 \\
\text{Closing cash balance} &= 4{,}800
\end{aligned}
\]
The firm's cash fell slightly because investing outflows outweighed the combined operating and financing inflows. However, the operating cash flow remains positive, suggesting the core business still generates cash.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Classify each of the following transactions as operating, investing or financing activity and indicate whether it is a cash inflow or outflow:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Purchase of raw materials on credit.
  \item Sale of a piece of equipment for cash.
  \item Issuance of a five‑year bond for cash.
  \item Payment of cash dividends to shareholders.
  \item Receipt of cash from customers for services rendered.
\end{enumerate}
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item A manufacturing firm receives \$120{,}000 from customers, pays \$70{,}000 to suppliers, \$15{,}000 in wages, and \$5{,}000 in taxes. Compute the net cash flow from operating activities.
  \item The same firm buys a new machine for \$45{,}000 and sells an old forklift for \$8{,}000. Determine the net cash flow from investing activities.
  \item The firm issues new shares and receives \$30{,}000, repays a bank loan of \$12{,}000, and pays a dividend of \$4{,}000. Calculate the net cash flow from financing activities.
  \item Using the three net cash figures you obtained, find the overall net change in cash for the period.
  \item Explain why a company might have a negative net cash flow from investing activities while still being financially healthy.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Operating activities & Cash inflows and outflows directly tied to the day‑to‑day production, sales and delivery of goods or services. \\
Investing activities & Cash flows resulting from the purchase or sale of long‑term assets and investments that are expected to provide benefits beyond the current period. \\
Financing activities & Cash flows that arise from changes in the firm's equity and debt structure, including borrowing, repaying, issuing shares and paying dividends. \\
Cash flow statement & Financial report that summarises cash inflows and outflows by operating, investing and financing categories for a specific period. \\
Net cash change & The sum of cash provided by (or used in) operating, investing and financing activities; indicates the increase or decrease in the cash balance. \\
Depreciation & A non‑cash expense that allocates the cost of a long‑term asset over its useful life; added back when converting profit to cash flow. \\
Treasury stock & Shares that a company has repurchased and holds; the cash paid for treasury stock is a financing outflow. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
